    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d6">

                <pb facs="0042" n="12" xml:id="pg12"/>

                <head>6. Memorandum From the President’s Special Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Bundy) to Secretary of State Rusk, Secretary of the Fowler Treasury,
                    and Secretary of Defense McNamara<anchor n="1" xml:id="d6-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-05-08">May 8, 1964</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>German Offset Purchases and Troop Strength</item>
                </list>
                <p>Following a discussion by the Cabinet Committee on Balance of Payments of German
                    offset purchases as related to U.S. troop deployments in Germany, the President
                    summarized the United States position as follows:<anchor n="2" xml:id="d6-f2"/>
                    <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>The U.S. supports the existing arrangements for its six-division force
                            in Germany.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>These existing arrangements, however, have two components: offset
                            purchases in the previously agreed amounts and U.S. force levels.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>If the Germans unilaterally alter the status quo by not living up to
                            their offset commitments to us, we shall be forced to reconsider the
                            question of U.S. force levels.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>During his next visit to Germany, Defense Secretary McNamara is to
                            make certain that there is no misunderstanding on the part of the German
                            Government with respect to the above.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d6-f3"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">McGeorge Bundy</hi>
                        <anchor n="4" xml:id="d6-f4"/>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d6-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d6-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Subject File, Balance of
                    Payments, Vol. 1, November 27, 1963 thru October 31, 1964, Box 1.
                    Confidential.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d6-f2" n="2" xml:id="d6-f2-content">
                <p>The President met with the Cabinet Committee on Balance of Payments on May 4. No
                    minutes of this meeting have been found, but for Secretary Dillon’s memorandum
                    to the President in preparation for the meeting, see <ref target="#d4">Document
                        4</ref>. For the Cabinet Committee report that was discussed at the meeting,
                    see <ref target="#d5">Document 5</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d6-f3" n="3" xml:id="d6-f3-content">
                <p>Secretary of Defense McNamara visited Bonn May 9–11 to discuss free world defense
                    policies with German Defense Minister Von Hassel.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d6-f4" n="4" xml:id="d6-f4-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that indicates Bundy signed the original.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
